Dispute 21 - Day 3
What You Need to Know About The Collection of Expired Debt

Bruce,

Case law is defined as "the law as established by the outcome of former
cases." 

In today's lesson we're focusing on Debt's outside of the Statute
of Limitations in relation to the Law and various court decisions in
regards to such debts. In day 2 we discussed tackling collection
accounts through the process of Debt Validation. As mentioned in that
lesson, one action you should always take as the repair specialist when
disputing a collection account and requesting Debt Validation is to
determine whether the account is inside or outside of the Statute of
Limitations. This is a very important and should be a non-negotiable in
your dispute process, as court rulings have given us valuable insight on
ways to deal with debt that is outside of the statute of limitations. 

**(For the sake of this lesson, the term "Time-barred" refers to debt
that is outside of the statute of limitations.)** 

Today, we will take a look at case law and review court rulings that
tell us how and why collecting on time-barred debt is or isn't a
violation of a consumer's FDCPA rights. I encourage you to read this
lesson all the way through, jotting down any questions or thoughts that
come up along the way and then go back through the lesson in search for
your answers. Also, it will be beneficial to follow-up this lesson by
conducting further research of the court cases presented below and
reviewing some or all of them in detail to gain knowledge and evidence
you can use when tackling collection debts. 

Each case included gives you a synopsis of the court's ruling, which
is verbiage you can use in your dispute and validation letters as
leverage when fighting collection agencies on time barred debt. In some
instances, merely mentioning to a collection agency the ways in which
they are violating your (or your client's) rights based on what the
courts have determined in similar cases is enough to get them off of
your back and cease collection activity. 

The Purpose of a Statute of Limitation (SOL)

The purpose of a statute of limitation is to require diligent
prosecution of known claims. Statutes help ensure claims and
controversies are brought and resolved while evidence and memories are
relatively fresh. Statutes of limitations protect defendants from the
burden of having to respond to stale claims. 

All states have statutes of limitation that limit the amount of time in
which a creditor, its assignee, debt collector or debt purchaser has to
file a lawsuit to collect on a particular type of debt. These time
periods vary from state to state.

How The FDCPA Changed the Way Debts Outside of the SOL are Handled

Historically, neither a creditor, nor its agents (including its
attorneys) owed any obligation during the collection process
(pre-litigation, or even after suit was filed) to advise a debtor that
the debt being collected upon was beyond the statute of limitation -
that is, the statute of limitations for suing on the debt had accrued
and expired.

This has changed with the adoption of the Fair Debt Collection Practices
Act (FDCPA), 15 U.S.C. § 1692, et seq. Today, there is a growing body
of decisional authority interpreting the appropriateness of collection
activities on consumer debt, regulated by the FDCPA, when the statute of
limitations on the debt has expired.

Many federal courts have issued decisions holding that suing, or even
collecting (without suit), on debt outside of the statute of limitations
(AKA "time-barred debt") may violate the FDCPA under a variety of
theories, such as: 

·         Collection of time-barred debt is unconscionable (15
U.S.C. § 1692f), is neither authorized, nor permitted by law 
AND
·         Collection of time-barred debt is misleading in
various ways (§ 1692e(2), § 1692e(5) and § 1692e(10)).

The trend of these decisions can be gleaned from the following cases:

Filgueiras v. Portfolio Recovery Assocs., Inc., 2016 WL 1626958 (D. N.J.
April 25, 2016)

* "While appropriate for a debt collector to request voluntary repayment
of a time-barred debt, if the statute of limitations is unclear, a
letter that presents "settlement options" is potentially misleading, and
states a prima facie FDCPA claim, and motion to dismiss reversed.

* Another letter that stated "settlement in full," however, did not
violate FDCPA where debtor was notified "because of age of your debt, we
will not sue you for it." See Tatis v. Allied Interstate, LLC, 2016 WL
5660431 (9/29/2016)

Buchanan v. Northland Group, Inc., 776 F.3d 393, 395 (6th Cir. 2015)
* "Settlement offer made on time-barred debt; this settlement offer
creates confusion to the least sophisticated debtor; the use of the term
"settlement offer" falsely implies that payment could be compelled
through litigation."

McMahon v. LVNV Funding, 

**LLC**, 744 F.3d 1010, 1020, 1021 (7th Cir. 2014)
* "If the debt collector uses language in its dunning letter that would
mislead an unsophisticated consumer into believing that the debt is
legally enforceable, regardless of whether the letter actually threatens
litigation, the collector has violated the FDCPA."

****Larsen v. JBC Legal Corp., P.C. 533 F.Supp.2d 290, 301-03 (E.D. N.Y.
2008)* "Threatening legal action on time-barred debt violates FDCPA"

Beattie v. D.M. Collections, Inc., 754 F.Supp. 383, 393 (D. Del. 1991)
* "Threatening of a lawsuit which the debt collector knows or should
know is unavailable or unwinnable by reason of a legal bar such as the
statute of limitations is the kind of abusive practice the FDCPA was
intended to eliminate."

****Kimber v. Fed. Fin. Corp., 668 F.Supp. 1480, 1487 (M.D. Ala. 1987)*
"A debt collector's filing of a lawsuit on a debt that appears to be
time barred, without the debt collector having first determined after a
reasonable inquiry that that limitations period has been or should be
tolled, is an unfair and unconscionable means of collecting the debt."

Let's take a look at Kimber v. Fed. Fin. Corp.

Starting in 1987, with the case of Kimber v. Federal Financial Corp.,
668 F.Supp. 1480 (M.D. Ala. 1987), the District Court early on, first
addressed whether the threat to, and actual filing of, a collection suit
on time-barred debt violated the FDCPA.

In Kimber, the debtor incurred a debt to a retail store that became
overdue in September of 1975. In 1976, the debt was purchased by the
Federal Financial Corporation ("FFC"). In 1984, FFC assigned the account
to an attorney for collection. In 1985, during a telephone call with the
attorney's office, the debtor was informed the now-10-year-old debt
needed to be paid, and she was threatened with a collection suit. She
refused to pay; suit was actually filed by FFC in Alabama state court.

The debtor answered the complaint and raised the statute of limitations
as a defense. The state court dismissed the case as untimely because the
longest possible limitations period (6 years) had long expired by the
time the action was filed in 1985. 

Subsequently, the debtor filed an action in federal court under the
FDCPA, claiming that FFC's filing of a clearly time-barred lawsuit
violates §§ 1692e and 1692f of the FDCPA, as unfair, unconscionable
and deceptive means of collecting the debt.  The Court
agreed.  Kimber, supra, 668 F.Supp. at 1487.  The Court wrote, in
pertinent part:

The court agrees with Kimber that a debt collector's filing of a
lawsuit on a debt that appears time barred without the debt collector
having first determined after reasonable inquiry that the limitations
period has been or should be tolled is an unfair and unconscionable
means of collecting the debt. . ."  (Kimber, supra, 668 F.Supp. at
1487.) 

The District Court explained its reasoning as follows:
Time barred lawsuits are, absent tolling, unjust and unfair as a matter
of public policy, and this is no less true in the consumer context.  As
with any defendant sued on a stale claim, the passage of time, not only
dulls the consumer's memory of the circumstances and validity of the
debt, but heightens the probability that she will no longer have
personal records detailing the status of the debt.  

Significantly, the Kimber court rejected any argument "the statute of
limitations is an affirmative defense which is waived if not raised
[and] a [collector] plaintiff may not be penalized for knowingly filing
a time barred suit."  The court's wholly unconvincing rationale for
rejecting this argument was that Rule 11 of the Federal Rules of Civil
Procedure permitted the imposition of sanctions against an attorney who
knowingly filed a time barred suit in federal court.  

What Do the Courts Say for Debts Outside of the SOL When the Threat of
Litigation is not Expressed...

While we've taken a look at ways in which collecting on a time-barred
debt by means of filing suit or threatening to take legal action has
been considered a violation of the FDCPA by the courts, it is important
to note that Since Kimber, numerous federal courts have weighed in on
this issue. The following cases give insight as to when collection
activity on a debt outside of the statute of limitations is 

**not** a violation of the FDCPA.  

In Freyermuth v. Credit Bureau Svcs, Inc., 248 F.3d 767, 771 (8th Cir.
2001), the Eighth Circuit held the expiration of the statute of
limitations did not eliminate the debt, 

**but only the remedies available (including filing suit)**. So
basically, if certain actions are not taken, such as seeking a judgment,
Collection activity could proceed on debt outside of the SOL.

Freyermuth v. Credit Bureau Servs., Inc., 248 F.3d 767, 771 (8th Cir.
2001)
* "In the absence of a threat of litigation or actual litigation, no
violation of the FDCPA has occurred when a debt collector attempts to
collect on a potentially time-barred debt that is otherwise valid."

Wallace v. Capital One Bank, 168 F.Supp.2d 526, 527-29 (D. Md. 2001) 
* "Debt validation notices that were silent as to whether debt was time
barred but which did not threaten collection action did not violate
FDCPA."

Shorty v. Capital One Bank, 90 F.Supp.2d 1330, 1331-33 (D.N.M. 2000)
* "Sending a debt validation notice regarding time-barred debt, without
more, does not misrepresent the status of the debt and does not violate
the FDCPA" 

****

Huertas v. Galaxy Asset Mgmt., 641 F.3d 28, 33-34 (3rd Cir. 2011)

* "FDCPA not violated by collection activity on time-barred debt where
letter merely asked the debtor to "resolve" the issue, but did not
provide for or offer a "settlement"

What Happens When the "Threat" is not Direct but Implied

Even where the "threat" of litigation is not expressed or direct, an
implied threat may violate the FDCPA.  

****Huertas****, supra, 641 F.3d at 33-34; Perretta v. Capital
Acquisitions and Mgmt. Co., 2003 WL 21383757 (N.D. Cal. 2003)
* §   FDCPA claim stated where collection letter stated that failure
to work with collector could lead to non-specified "further steps being
taken"

****Stepney v. Outsourcing Solutions, Inc., 1997 WL 722972 at *4-5 (N.D.
Ill. 1997) * §   FDCPA claim stated where collection letter stated if
debt paid "no further collection action" would be taken.

Thus, while the mere attempt to collect a voluntary payment on an
out-of-statute debt may not violate the FDCPA, threatening the consumer
implicitly or expressly, directly or indirectly, with a lawsuit
concerning a debt beyond the statute of limitations has been found to
violate the FDCPA.

These courts have found that the act of threatening or filing lawsuits
on debts that are beyond the statute of limitations violate, inter alia,
15 U.S.C. § 1692f(1).

Courts have also found that threatening to take legal action when the
debt has gone beyond the statutory time limit constitutes a  false
representation of the status of a debt, in violation of §
1692e(2)(A),² § 1692e(5)³ and/or 1692e(10).⁴

Bruce, hopefully today's lesson has shed some light on the Statue
of Limitations and when a request for collection of a debt beyond the
statute of limitations (*aka* Time-barred) is and isn't considered a
violation of consumer rights, based on case law. I encourage you to
follow-up this lesson with further research of historical case law to
help deepen your understanding of the law and the courts interpretation
on the consumer laws that we use to assist our clients. If you find a
case that relates to a particular client's situation and the ruling was
in the consumer's favor, you can always reference that case and the
decision of the courts when disputing debts with a collection agency as
well as any FCRA cases with the Credit bureaus AFTER Round 1 and AFTER
the "alleged debt has been verified. Based on experience, I advise that
you limit your mention of legal references to little or none in a Round
1 Letter. We will discuss more about the "Why" when we move into the
FCRA section of the course that deals with the dispute process and
dispute letter writing.  

